**Corollary 10.4 (extension of the inherited selected Pareto vectors).** For a selected original history $h=(y,e,n)$ put $v_h=C_gv_y$ and retain the inherited vector

$$
V^h_{h'}=\operatorname{tr}(\Theta(v_hv_h^T)\rho_{h'})
        =\|K(v_h)U_{e',n'}a_{y'}\|^2
\quad(h'=(y',e',n')).
\tag{PG.58}
$$

For every $s\le s_y^{\max}$ this entire vector is attained by one design selected by $h$. It is coordinatewise least among all resource vectors of designs minimizing coordinate $h$ at that strength. It is their unique Pareto vector and is globally Pareto against the entire finite complex class.

Proof. The construction (PG.38) has acquired effect $E\Theta(v_hv_h^T)E^\dagger$ independently of strength, so it attains (PG.58) on all twelve inputs. Any selected acquired minimizer has high coefficient $P=v_hv_h^T$ by the scalar certificate. Lemma 3.1 forces its full acquired effect to dominate that supported effect, so every original coordinate is at least $V^h$. This proves leastness. Any design weakly lowering the entire vector must still minimize coordinate $h$; the same domination forbids a strict decrease elsewhere. This proves global Pareto status without asserting that all selected minimizers have the same acquired operator.

These are RG's existing vectors with enlarged attainment intervals. RG's separate assertion that they lie outside all three sharp-average equality faces retains its original interval
$\min\{\sqrt\ell/2,s_5^{\rm RG},s_{16}^{\rm RG},s_{27}^{\rm RG}\}$,
where each $s_y^{\rm RG}$ is its saturated sufficient cap with $1/2$ instead of the first entry $1$ in (PG.36). No extension of that separation statement is made here. Equality of twelve separately optimized values cannot assemble twelve different selected instruments into one simultaneous optimizer. $\square$

